WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Drag across a whole phrase in Ayat al-Kursi: يَعْلَمُ مَا بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَمَا خَلْفَهُمْ.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:28.228
Then ask what it means in classical Arabic, and where else the Quran uses it.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:01:28.843
Literally “between their hands” and “behind them”: the scholars
read it as place, as time, and as the seen and the unseen.

00:01:28.843 --> 00:01:42.843
In Saba 34:9 it's literal: the sky and earth in front of them and behind them.

00:01:42.843 --> 00:02:05.677
In As-Saff 61:6, “between my hands” means the Torah that came before.

00:02:05.677 --> 00:02:21.852
Then we ask for other idioms whose literal picture isn't the meaning.

00:02:21.852 --> 00:02:52.236
A hand chained to the neck, the wing of humility, ears struck into a long sleep.

00:02:52.236 --> 00:03:20.236
In Maryam 19:4, the word cards show the picture: a head that catches fire with grey.

00:03:20.236 --> 00:03:24.486
Read. Select. Ask. Discover.
